tags: event

= Understanding the AfD

The AfD has grown from a party of eurosceptic professors into the strongest opposition party, and in parts of the East into the strongest party of all. The party's own commission has collected the research.

Economists point to the regions that have been left behind: closed plants, empty town centres, the last bus at six. Where people fear for their status, the AfD grows.

Political scientists see a cultural backlash: against migration, against climate policy, against a feeling of being lectured by the cities. Economic aid alone, they say, will not win these voters back.

Researchers from the East stress the experience after 1990: mass unemployment, westerners in every leading post, and a lasting feeling of being second-class citizens.

Communication experts point to TikTok and YouTube, where the AfD reaches more young people than all other parties together, with short, emotional videos.

There is probably some truth in all of this. Whatever the case, we must do something.

- @p1: AfD voters have made their choice. We should focus on mobilising our own voters.
- @p2: We must become a broad people's party again, and compete for the voters of the centre.
- @p3: We must learn to campaign online, with emotion as well as arguments.
- @p4: We must win back the workers and the people in the regions left behind.

@p1
on-arrival: left_dissent -= 2; new_middle_spd += 1*(1-dissent)

We will not chase the AfD's voters. We focus on getting our own supporters to the polls.

@p2
on-arrival: peoples_party_support += 1; center_strength += 5; reformist_strength += 2; left_strength -= 3; left_dissent += 4; labor_dissent += 2

We set out to win back the centre: the self-employed, the suburbs and the small towns. [? if peoples_party = 0 : The left and the AfA are sceptical, and want a party that is clearly on the side of working people. ?]

@p3
on-arrival: center_strength += 5; afd_urgency += 1; radio += 1 if radio = 0

We hire young creators and start a fast, emotional campaign on the platforms where the AfD is strongest. Time will tell how effective it is.

@p4
on-arrival: left_strength += 3; center_strength += 3; labor_strength += 4; left_dissent -= 3; center_dissent -= 3; labor_dissent -= 3; workers_spd += 2*(1-dissent); unemployed_spd += 2*(1-dissent); catholics_spd += 1*(1-dissent)

We send our people back into the factories, the job centres and the towns of the East, to listen before we talk.
